88 17592186045417 true] [17592186436041 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436035 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436041 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436037 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436041 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436041 1077 "selection" 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436041 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436034 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436041 1079 #uuid "ee7f980b-3738-5f95-89d8-bd69eb485ba5" 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436041 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436042 1069 "q-80505" 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436042 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436042 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436042 1072 #uuid "34094a62-379f-5750-81e2-774654924818" 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436042 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/c5/c50e0e01d432b1db736fad09dd5f5b82286e981d88586e848d9b57c8a9910eb0.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle II does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles I and III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436043 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436040 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436043 1069 "q-86864" 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436043 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436043 1071 "![](images/bb/bbc1bc7b3bb910ffbd13571283bbfa305404d13eb2cb3b5ab423a1c02e46ab86.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. ${∬}_{R}y({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,2]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,2]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$" 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436043 1072 #uuid "f7e910b3-a1a9-5ea8-8009-6cb31a154137" 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436043 1074 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436044 1058 #edb/ref 17592186436045 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592186436044 1062 #uuid "151f4abe-23c3-58b1-8e53-69be8020bff5" 13194139534788 17592186045417 true] [17592